// src/user/compositor/surface_buffer_bind.pdx -- Wave0-B6-04 (paideia-os #2252)
//
// SurfaceBufferBind -- the (buffer, wait_timeline/value, signal_timeline/value)
// staging record accumulated on a KIND_SURFACE_COMMIT (see #2248) between the
// client's buffer-attach RPC and the compositor's atomic commit consume. This
// module is P1's structural mitigation on the PWP hot path: every buffer
// handoff carries an explicit (timeline_id, value) pair on BOTH the pull side
// (compositor waits before it reads) and the push side (compositor signals
// when it is done reading, so the client's rendering pipeline can recycle the
// buffer). Implicit-fence fallback is banned -- see
// design/roadmap/next-wave-synthesis.md §7 row P1 and
// design/compositor/pwp-spec-vocabulary.md §2.1..§2.2.
//
// =============================================================================
// SECTION 0  VALUE TYPE, NOT A KIND
// =============================================================================
//
// A SurfaceBufferBind is a *value* -- a 40-byte data shape that flows across
// a surface_commit call. It has no KIND_ID slot, no rights bitmask, no mint
// or revoke gate, and no cap-table row. The buffer being bound is itself a
// KIND_BUFFER capability (kernel-authored elsewhere); the timeline ids point
// at KIND_HW_TIMELINE / KIND_DISPLAY_TIMELINE / KIND_GPU_TIMELINE cap slots
// the caller already holds; damage_hint is either 0 (whole-surface repaint)
// or a KIND_DAMAGE_REGION slot the surface_commit dedup pass will resolve.
// So the wire-level unit of authority is the SURROUNDING surface_commit cap
// (which IS linear, per §2.2), not the bind record inside it. The distinction
// matters for the closed vocabulary commitment: minor-version field additions
// inside this record do not re-open the KIND_* enumeration, and no derived
// kind number needs to be reserved for a value-type shape (§P14).
//
// The staging row is a SINGLE per-module scratch cell: the pending
// bind record for the surface_commit currently under construction. This is
// safe under the compositor's single-writer discipline (P0 in
// pwp-spec-vocabulary.md §0 -- one svc-compositor process, one commit
// pipeline per surface, in-flight commits carried on their own LINEAR caps
// consumed at atomic transition). A future landing that promotes the
// staging cell to a per-surface pool (once surface_commit is minted per
// surface) will keep the same on-cap wire shape; the scratch remains a
// compat shim, not a design boundary. Callers that want independent
// buffer-bind values across concurrent construction sites must call the
// factory serially -- exactly the pattern the surface-commit RPC dispatch
// already enforces per surface endpoint.
//
// =============================================================================
// SECTION 1  P1: WHY (timeline_id, value) IS THE ONLY BIND SHAPE
// =============================================================================
//
// Wayland-on-NVIDIA taught the whole industry the "implicit dmabuf sync
// flicker class": a client attaches a buffer, the compositor reads it before
// the client's GPU has actually finished writing, and the frame tears or
// flickers -- silently, because there is no error path when the sync
// contract is implicit. The compositor cannot know whether the client's
// last GPU submission has completed without asking the driver, and the
// driver cannot tell the compositor without a fence, and there is no fence,
// because implicit sync is a fiction glued together by dmabuf poll-fds and
// driver-specific waiter promotion.
//
// PaideiaOS forecloses the whole class by construction. Every buffer-attach
// carries FOUR non-zero words: a wait_timeline (a timeline capability the
// compositor blocks on until wait_value is reached before it reads a single
// byte of the buffer) and a signal_timeline (a timeline capability the
// compositor writes signal_value to after it stops reading, so the client's
// producer pipeline knows when it is safe to recycle the underlying storage).
// Both wait AND signal are mandatory. A bind whose wait_timeline_id == 0 or
// whose signal_timeline_id == 0 fails the surface_buffer_attach_is_explicit_sync
// predicate the surface_commit consume path checks before it invokes any
// timeline wait syscall; commit refuses the whole transaction rather than
// fall back to a synchronous readback. That refusal is the load-bearing
// clause of P1 -- it removes the compositor's ability to compensate for a
// missing timeline by silently waiting, which is what dmabuf poll effectively
// did, which is what causes the flicker.
//
// The (timeline_id, u64_value) pair shape is the linux_drm_syncobj shape
// (Christian Koenig, AMD 2017; Wayland wp_linux_drm_syncobj_manager_v1),
// promoted here from a protocol-extension knob to a first-class field on
// the value type. That promotion IS the novel-clean move: on Linux the
// syncobj wait/signal words are optional negotiated tokens delivered by an
// opt-in ext-* protocol whose absence forces an implicit fallback; here
// the two pairs are mandatory columns of the value's byte layout, and
// there is no fallback wire path to fall back to. No wp-* / ext-* sidecar
// negotiation (P11); no wp_linux_drm_syncobj_manager_v1 analogue in the
// PWP vocabulary; the fields are always present because the record does
// not compile without them.
//
// =============================================================================
// SECTION 2  ON-WIRE RECORD LAYOUT (40 BYTES, u64 ALIGNED)
// =============================================================================
//
// Offsets are byte-relative from the start of the SurfaceBufferBind record;
// alignment is 8-byte for the u64 timeline pairs and packed 4-byte for the
// two u32 tail slots. Total size 40 bytes (aligned to 8; no tail padding
// because the u32 pair at offsets 32/36 exactly fills the last 8-byte word).
//
//   offset  size  field
//   ------  ----  ---------------------------------------------------------
//     0     u64   wait_timeline_id     ; KIND_HW_TIMELINE slot; 0 == BANNED
//     8     u64   wait_value           ; timeline value compositor waits for
//    16     u64   signal_timeline_id   ; KIND_HW_TIMELINE slot; 0 == BANNED
//    24     u64   signal_value         ; value compositor writes on release
//    32     u32   buffer_kind_id       ; KIND_BUFFER cap slot being attached
//    36     u32   damage_hint          ; KIND_DAMAGE_REGION slot; 0 == whole
//
// The wait pair is checked BEFORE the compositor reads the buffer; the
// signal pair is written AFTER the compositor stops reading, so ordering
// across the two pairs is (client_produce -> wait_value) then
// (compositor_read) then (compositor_write signal_value -> client_recycle).
// Value monotonicity across successive binds against the same timeline is a
// timeline-cap invariant (a KIND_HW_TIMELINE never regresses), not this
// module's concern.
//
// =============================================================================
// SECTION 3  FAILURE TAXONOMY 0xFFFFED80..0xFFFFED8F
// =============================================================================
//
// Codes live in the 0xFFFFED80 block (adjacent to HLG 0xFFFFED90 and HDR
// metadata 0xFFFFED70). Every routine returns 0 on success and one of these
// on failure; ok/err discipline is a strict single-return-slot rule (rax).

module SurfaceBufferBind = structure {

  pub let SBB_OK                        : u64 = 0
  pub let SBB_BAD_DEST                  : u64 = 0xFFFFED8F   // caller passed NULL / mis-aligned pointer
  pub let SBB_BAD_BUFFER                : u64 = 0xFFFFED8E   // buffer_kind_id truncated (upper 32 bits set)
  pub let SBB_BAD_WAIT_TIMELINE         : u64 = 0xFFFFED8D   // wait_timeline_id == 0 (P1 refuse)
  pub let SBB_BAD_SIGNAL_TIMELINE       : u64 = 0xFFFFED8C   // signal_timeline_id == 0 (P1 refuse)
  pub let SBB_BAD_DAMAGE                : u64 = 0xFFFFED8B   // damage_hint truncated (upper 32 bits set)

  // Field byte offsets. All accessors reference these constants rather than
  // materialise the numbers inline so a future layout change is a one-line
  // edit here.
  pub let SBB_OFF_WAIT_TIMELINE_ID      : u64 = 0
  pub let SBB_OFF_WAIT_VALUE            : u64 = 8
  pub let SBB_OFF_SIGNAL_TIMELINE_ID    : u64 = 16
  pub let SBB_OFF_SIGNAL_VALUE          : u64 = 24
  pub let SBB_OFF_BUFFER_KIND_ID        : u64 = 32
  pub let SBB_OFF_DAMAGE_HINT           : u64 = 36

  pub let SBB_RECORD_BYTES              : u64 = 40

  // u32 field mask -- values above 0xFFFFFFFF are refused with
  // SBB_BAD_BUFFER / SBB_BAD_DAMAGE rather than silently truncated.
  pub let SBB_U32_MAX                   : u64 = 0xFFFFFFFF

  // Damage-hint sentinel: 0 == "whole surface, no damage tracking hint".
  // Any non-zero value is a KIND_DAMAGE_REGION slot the commit path resolves.
  pub let SBB_DAMAGE_WHOLE_SURFACE      : u64 = 0

  // ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
  // Section 4  Per-module scratch cell.
  // ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
  //
  // ONE staging bind record (40 bytes, aligned to 8; declared as 5 u64
  // slots because the encoder does not have a `[u8; 40] @align(8)` byte-array
  // form separate from the 8-slot rows other modules use). Slot 4's low
  // 32 bits are buffer_kind_id; the high 32 bits are damage_hint. Accessors
  // extract via mov + shr / and.
  pub let mut _surface_buffer_bind_staging : [u64; 5] = uninit @align(8)

  // Counters (factory_ok / factory_refused / predicate_calls / damage_updates).
  pub let mut _surface_buffer_bind_stats   : [u64; 4] = uninit @align(64)

  pub let SBB_ST_FACTORY_OK      : u64 = 0
  pub let SBB_ST_FACTORY_REFUSED : u64 = 1
  pub let SBB_ST_PRED_CALLS      : u64 = 2
  pub let SBB_ST_DAMAGE_UPDATES  : u64 = 3

  // ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
  // Section 5  Fingerprint tag (value-type presence, not a mint tag).
  // ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
  //
  // Named per B6-04 task spec: pdx_kind_surface_buffer_bind_meta. Because
  // SurfaceBufferBind is a VALUE TYPE with no KIND_ID slot and no mint gate,
  // this tag records the module's data-shape presence rather than a
  // per-cap mint event. The surface_commit consume path emits the tag via
  // klog_s0 the first time a bind is presented on the wire post-boot; the
  // string needs allowlisting in tools/verify-fingerprint-coverage.sh until
  // the consume-path landing (G7.M2 close) wires the emitter.
  pub let pdx_kind_surface_buffer_bind_meta : [u8; 47] = "surface buffer bind meta [legacy: SBB META OK]\0"

  // ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
  // Section 6  Counter primitives.
  // ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

  pub let surface_buffer_bind_stats_reset : () -> () !{mem} @{} =
    fn () -> unsafe {
      effects: {mem}, capabilities: {},
      justification: "Wave0-B6-04 (#2252): clear the staging cell + counters at boot before any factory call. Load-modify-store discipline; no add reg,[mem].",
      block: {
        lea r10, [rip + _surface_buffer_bind_staging];
        xor rax, rax;
        xor rcx, rcx;
      sbbsr_stg:
        mov [r10 + rcx * 8], rax;
        add rcx, 1;
        cmp rcx, 5;
        jb  sbbsr_stg;
        lea r10, [rip + _surface_buffer_bind_stats];
        xor rcx, rcx;
      sbbsr_stat:
        mov [r10 + rcx * 8], rax;
        add rcx, 1;
        cmp rcx, 4;
        jb  sbbsr_stat;
        ret
      }
    }

  pub let surface_buffer_bind_note : (u64) -> () !{mem} @{} =
    fn (which: u64) -> unsafe {
      effects: {mem}, capabilities: {},
      justification: "Wave0-B6-04 (#2252): counter bump. Encoder-conservative: load-modify-store, no add reg,[mem], no test.",
      block: {
        cmp rdi, 4;
        jae sbbn_note_out;
        lea r10, [rip + _surface_buffer_bind_stats];
        mov rax, [r10 + rdi * 8];
        add rax, 1;
        mov [r10 + rdi * 8], rax;
      sbbn_note_out:
        ret
      }
    }

  pub let surface_buffer_bind_stat : (u64) -> u64 !{mem} @{} =
    fn (which: u64) -> unsafe {
      effects: {mem}, capabilities: {},
      justification: "Wave0-B6-04 (#2252): counter read.",
      block: {
        cmp rdi, 4;
        jae sbbst_zero;
        lea r10, [rip + _surface_buffer_bind_stats];
        mov rax, [r10 + rdi * 8];
        ret;
      sbbst_zero:
        xor rax, rax;
        ret
      }
    }

  // ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
  // Section 7  Factory: surface_buffer_attach_new.
  // ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
  //
  // surface_buffer_attach_new(buffer_cap, wait_timeline, wait_val,
  //                            signal_timeline, signal_val) -> u64
  //   Returns SBB_OK (0) on success -- the filled record is at
  //   _surface_buffer_bind_staging (accessed via
  //   surface_buffer_bind_staging_ptr). Damage_hint defaults to
  //   SBB_DAMAGE_WHOLE_SURFACE; call surface_buffer_attach_set_damage_hint
  //   after the factory to attach a KIND_DAMAGE_REGION slot.
  //   Returns a non-zero SBB_BAD_* on any of these P1 refusals:
  //     - wait_timeline == 0                     -> SBB_BAD_WAIT_TIMELINE
  //     - signal_timeline == 0                   -> SBB_BAD_SIGNAL_TIMELINE
  //     - buffer_cap upper 32 bits set (u32 fit) -> SBB_BAD_BUFFER
  //
  // The refusal ordering (wait, signal, then buffer) is deterministic so
  // the surface_commit consume path can log a single leaf reason. Five
  // SysV register args fit exactly: rdi/rsi/rdx/rcx/r8. Return in rax.
  // Five callee-save pushes keep rsp%16 == 0 across the surface_buffer_bind_note
  // call (odd-parity convention: entry rsp%16 == 8, 5 pushes -> +40 -> == 0).
  pub let surface_buffer_attach_new : (u64, u64, u64, u64, u64) -> u64 !{mem} @{} =
    fn (buffer_cap: u64) (wait_timeline: u64) (wait_val: u64)
       (signal_timeline: u64) (signal_val: u64) -> unsafe {
      effects: {mem}, capabilities: {},
      justification: "Wave0-B6-04 (#2252): P1 factory. Refuse (wait|signal)_timeline == 0 -- implicit sync is banned by construction. Refuse buffer_cap > u32 -- silent truncation would corrupt the wire record's cap slot. Damage_hint defaults to 0 (whole surface). Five-push discipline (rbx/r12/r13/r14/r15) keeps rsp%16 == 0 before surface_buffer_bind_note. Load-modify-store on the staging cell; no add reg,[mem], no test.",
      block: {
        push rbx;
        push r12;
        push r13;
        push r14;
        push r15;

        // Hoist args into callee-save regs so the surface_buffer_bind_note
        // tail call does not clobber them.
        mov r12, rdi;   // buffer_cap
        mov r13, rsi;   // wait_timeline
        mov r14, rdx;   // wait_val
        mov r15, rcx;   // signal_timeline
        mov rbx, r8;    // signal_val

        // P1 refusal (a): wait_timeline_id must be non-zero.
        cmp r13, 0;
        je  sbbn_bad_wait;

        // P1 refusal (b): signal_timeline_id must be non-zero.
        cmp r15, 0;
        je  sbbn_bad_signal;

        // u32 fit: buffer_cap upper 32 bits must be clear.
        mov rax, 0xFFFFFFFF;
        cmp r12, rax;
        ja  sbbn_bad_buffer;

        // Populate the staging cell.
        lea r10, [rip + _surface_buffer_bind_staging];
        mov [r10 + 0],  r13;    // wait_timeline_id  @ SBB_OFF_WAIT_TIMELINE_ID
        mov [r10 + 8],  r14;    // wait_value        @ SBB_OFF_WAIT_VALUE
        mov [r10 + 16], r15;    // signal_timeline_id @ SBB_OFF_SIGNAL_TIMELINE_ID
        mov [r10 + 24], rbx;    // signal_value      @ SBB_OFF_SIGNAL_VALUE

        // Slot 4 packs {damage_hint:u32 high, buffer_kind_id:u32 low}.
        // Factory zeroes damage_hint (whole-surface default); accessor
        // surface_buffer_attach_set_damage_hint updates only the high half.
        mov rax, r12;
        and rax, 0xFFFFFFFF;    // low 32 = buffer_kind_id; high 32 = 0 (damage_hint)
        mov [r10 + 32], rax;

        mov rdi, 0;             // SBB_ST_FACTORY_OK
        call surface_buffer_bind_note;
        xor rax, rax;           // SBB_OK
        jmp sbbn_out;

      sbbn_bad_wait:
        mov rdi, 1;             // SBB_ST_FACTORY_REFUSED
        call surface_buffer_bind_note;
        mov rax, 0xFFFFED8D;    // SBB_BAD_WAIT_TIMELINE
        jmp sbbn_out;

      sbbn_bad_signal:
        mov rdi, 1;             // SBB_ST_FACTORY_REFUSED
        call surface_buffer_bind_note;
        mov rax, 0xFFFFED8C;    // SBB_BAD_SIGNAL_TIMELINE
        jmp sbbn_out;

      sbbn_bad_buffer:
        mov rdi, 1;             // SBB_ST_FACTORY_REFUSED
        call surface_buffer_bind_note;
        mov rax, 0xFFFFED8E;    // SBB_BAD_BUFFER

      sbbn_out:
        pop r15;
        pop r14;
        pop r13;
        pop r12;
        pop rbx;
        ret
      }
    }

  // ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
  // Section 8  Staging cell pointer -- lets callers pass the filled record
  // into surface_commit accumulate without exposing the underlying storage.
  // ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

  pub let surface_buffer_bind_staging_ptr : () -> u64 !{mem} @{} =
    fn () -> unsafe {
      effects: {mem}, capabilities: {},
      justification: "Wave0-B6-04 (#2252): return &_surface_buffer_bind_staging so surface_commit consume can read the filled record without a copy. No safety check -- the cell exists for the whole process lifetime.",
      block: {
        lea rax, [rip + _surface_buffer_bind_staging];
        ret
      }
    }

  // ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
  // Section 9  Predicate: surface_buffer_attach_is_explicit_sync.
  // ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
  //
  // Returns 1 iff BOTH wait_timeline_id AND signal_timeline_id are non-zero
  // in the record pointed to by rdi. Returns 0 iff either is zero -- exactly
  // the case P1 bans and the surface_commit consume path must refuse rather
  // than silently wait. Also returns 0 for a NULL pointer so that a defensive
  // caller (surface_commit BEFORE it looks at any field) is safe against a
  // mis-wired record without needing its own null check.
  //
  // The predicate is intentionally read-only: it never mutates the staging
  // cell and never bumps the refused counter -- refusal accounting lives on
  // the factory side (where the caller is trying to CONSTRUCT an invalid
  // record) and on the surface_commit consume side (where the caller has
  // presented one). This routine is a pure gate for the consume-side check.
  //
  // The `cmp reg,0 / je` pattern is used in place of the `test reg,reg / jz`
  // idiom because this codebase's encoder gate refuses `test` (see the
  // wave-0 encoder-conservative note in hlg_transform.pdx surface). Three
  // callee-save pushes keep the odd-parity convention even though no nested
  // call is made -- future counter-bump hooks can be added without touching
  // the frame size.
  pub let surface_buffer_attach_is_explicit_sync : (u64) -> u64 !{mem} @{} =
    fn (op: u64) -> unsafe {
      effects: {mem}, capabilities: {},
      justification: "Wave0-B6-04 (#2252): P1 predicate. Returns 1 iff wait_timeline_id != 0 AND signal_timeline_id != 0. NULL pointer returns 0 (defensive). Bumps SBB_ST_PRED_CALLS. Three-push discipline (rbx/r12/r13) leaves rsp%16 == 0 before the note call. Uses cmp reg,0 / je -- no test.",
      block: {
        push rbx;
        push r12;
        push r13;

        mov r12, rdi;
        cmp r12, 0;
        je  sbbi_no;

        // wait_timeline_id @ offset 0.
        mov rax, [r12 + 0];
        cmp rax, 0;
        je  sbbi_no;

        // signal_timeline_id @ offset 16.
        mov rax, [r12 + 16];
        cmp rax, 0;
        je  sbbi_no;

        mov rdi, 2;             // SBB_ST_PRED_CALLS
        call surface_buffer_bind_note;
        mov rax, 1;
        jmp sbbi_out;

      sbbi_no:
        mov rdi, 2;             // SBB_ST_PRED_CALLS
        call surface_buffer_bind_note;
        xor rax, rax;

      sbbi_out:
        pop r13;
        pop r12;
        pop rbx;
        ret
      }
    }

  // ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
  // Section 10  Damage-hint updater.
  // ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
  //
  // surface_buffer_attach_set_damage_hint(op_ptr, damage_id) -> u64
  //   Rewrites the high 32 bits of slot 4 with damage_id. Refuses
  //   damage_id > u32 with SBB_BAD_DAMAGE; refuses a NULL op_ptr with
  //   SBB_BAD_DEST. Setting damage_id back to 0 restores the whole-surface
  //   default. Preserves the low 32 bits (buffer_kind_id) with a
  //   load-mask-or-store sequence.
  pub let surface_buffer_attach_set_damage_hint : (u64, u64) -> u64 !{mem} @{} =
    fn (op: u64) (damage_id: u64) -> unsafe {
      effects: {mem}, capabilities: {},
      justification: "Wave0-B6-04 (#2252): update damage_hint in slot 4 high half without perturbing buffer_kind_id in slot 4 low half. Reject NULL / >u32. Load-mask-or-store; no add reg,[mem], no test. Three-push discipline.",
      block: {
        push rbx;
        push r12;
        push r13;

        mov r12, rdi;
        mov r13, rsi;

        cmp r12, 0;
        je  sbbd_bad_dest;

        mov rax, 0xFFFFFFFF;
        cmp r13, rax;
        ja  sbbd_bad_damage;

        // Load slot 4, clear high 32 bits, OR in damage_id << 32.
        mov rax, [r12 + 32];
        mov rcx, 0xFFFFFFFF;
        and rax, rcx;           // keep buffer_kind_id (low 32)
        mov rcx, r13;
        shl rcx, 32;
        or  rax, rcx;
        mov [r12 + 32], rax;

        mov rdi, 3;             // SBB_ST_DAMAGE_UPDATES
        call surface_buffer_bind_note;
        xor rax, rax;           // SBB_OK
        jmp sbbd_out;

      sbbd_bad_dest:
        mov rax, 0xFFFFED8F;    // SBB_BAD_DEST
        jmp sbbd_out;

      sbbd_bad_damage:
        mov rax, 0xFFFFED8B;    // SBB_BAD_DAMAGE

      sbbd_out:
        pop r13;
        pop r12;
        pop rbx;
        ret
      }
    }

  // ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
  // Section 11  Field accessors.
  // ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
  //
  // Six read-only getters, one per field. Each takes op_ptr in rdi and
  // returns the field in rax; a NULL op_ptr returns 0 so a defensive
  // caller does not need to null-check. u32 fields (buffer_kind_id at
  // offset 32 low half; damage_hint at offset 32 high half) are extracted
  // with mov + and / shr.

  pub let surface_buffer_attach_wait_timeline_id : (u64) -> u64 !{mem} @{} =
    fn (op: u64) -> unsafe {
      effects: {mem}, capabilities: {},
      justification: "Wave0-B6-04 (#2252): read wait_timeline_id @ offset 0. NULL -> 0.",
      block: {
        cmp rdi, 0;
        je  sbbg_wti_zero;
        mov rax, [rdi + 0];
        ret;
      sbbg_wti_zero:
        xor rax, rax;
        ret
      }
    }

  pub let surface_buffer_attach_wait_value : (u64) -> u64 !{mem} @{} =
    fn (op: u64) -> unsafe {
      effects: {mem}, capabilities: {},
      justification: "Wave0-B6-04 (#2252): read wait_value @ offset 8. NULL -> 0.",
      block: {
        cmp rdi, 0;
        je  sbbg_wv_zero;
        mov rax, [rdi + 8];
        ret;
      sbbg_wv_zero:
        xor rax, rax;
        ret
      }
    }

  pub let surface_buffer_attach_signal_timeline_id : (u64) -> u64 !{mem} @{} =
    fn (op: u64) -> unsafe {
      effects: {mem}, capabilities: {},
      justification: "Wave0-B6-04 (#2252): read signal_timeline_id @ offset 16. NULL -> 0.",
      block: {
        cmp rdi, 0;
        je  sbbg_sti_zero;
        mov rax, [rdi + 16];
        ret;
      sbbg_sti_zero:
        xor rax, rax;
        ret
      }
    }

  pub let surface_buffer_attach_signal_value : (u64) -> u64 !{mem} @{} =
    fn (op: u64) -> unsafe {
      effects: {mem}, capabilities: {},
      justification: "Wave0-B6-04 (#2252): read signal_value @ offset 24. NULL -> 0.",
      block: {
        cmp rdi, 0;
        je  sbbg_sv_zero;
        mov rax, [rdi + 24];
        ret;
      sbbg_sv_zero:
        xor rax, rax;
        ret
      }
    }

  pub let surface_buffer_attach_buffer_kind_id : (u64) -> u64 !{mem} @{} =
    fn (op: u64) -> unsafe {
      effects: {mem}, capabilities: {},
      justification: "Wave0-B6-04 (#2252): read buffer_kind_id from slot 4 low half. NULL -> 0. Mask via and rax, 0xFFFFFFFF.",
      block: {
        cmp rdi, 0;
        je  sbbg_bk_zero;
        mov rax, [rdi + 32];
        mov rcx, 0xFFFFFFFF;
        and rax, rcx;
        ret;
      sbbg_bk_zero:
        xor rax, rax;
        ret
      }
    }

  pub let surface_buffer_attach_damage_hint : (u64) -> u64 !{mem} @{} =
    fn (op: u64) -> unsafe {
      effects: {mem}, capabilities: {},
      justification: "Wave0-B6-04 (#2252): read damage_hint from slot 4 high half. NULL -> 0. Extract via shr rax, 32.",
      block: {
        cmp rdi, 0;
        je  sbbg_dh_zero;
        mov rax, [rdi + 32];
        shr rax, 32;
        ret;
      sbbg_dh_zero:
        xor rax, rax;
        ret
      }
    }
}
